The Neon tileset
================

One silhouette, one colour, and the colour is data.

    scripts/tiles/build-neon.py            build the sheet and the prf
    scripts/tiles/build-neon.py --check    measure the palette and the output
    scripts/tiles/build-neon.py --preview  also write a mock map to look at

Registered as graphics mode 7 in lib/tiles/list.txt.  Modes 5 and 6 are
Shockbolt's and stay retired.

What it covers
--------------

    105 silhouettes  ->  2092 preference lines

    monsters      1013 lines    every monster in the game
    terrain        168 lines    all 42 features, four lighting variants each
    objects        157 lines    every object that carries its own colour
    flavours       302 lines    every flavour in flavor.txt
    traps          152 lines    38 of 40 traps, four variants each
    spell effects  300 lines    60 projections x 5 directions

Two traps are deliberately absent: 'no trap' and 'door lock', neither of which
is a thing drawn on the floor.

The 8x8 set spends 945 distinct pictures and covers none of ZangbandTK's
imported monsters, which is what graf-ztk.prf exists to paper over.  This set
needs no graf-ztk.prf: nothing is borrowed, because nothing is missing.

Why it is built this way
------------------------

Every other tileset paints colour into its art, and the game then discards its
own: once a graphic tile is assigned, ui-map.c:258 short-circuits on
`da & 0x80` and a monster's `color:` never reaches the screen.  So the twelve
zephyr hounds -- which differ by nothing else -- need twelve drawings there,
and get whichever ones somebody made.

Here the sheet has *the colour on the row and the shape on the column*, so a
preference line is (this thing's colour, this thing's silhouette) and both
halves are read out of lib/gamedata: a monster's `base:` and `color:`, a
feature's `graphics:`, a trap's, a flavour's, a projection's.  Twelve hounds
are one drawing.  Sixty spell elements are five.

It falls out of the prf format, which has always been `attr:char`, and in
graphics mode has always meant `row:col`.

Where the art lives
-------------------

`shapes/*.txt`, as sixteen lines of sixteen characters, `X` for ink.  Text
because it diffs in git, reviews without a paint program, and can be fixed by
hand in thirty seconds.  The sheet is a build artifact; delete it and rebuild.

Adding a shape is two edits: draw `shapes/<name>.txt`, then name it in
`manifest.txt` against a `base:`, a `feat:` code, a `tval:`, a `trap:` code or
a `gf:` direction.  Anything not named there has no tile and renders as its
ASCII letter, which is the intended state of an unfinished set and the same
argument this directory's other README makes about uniques: a letter says
"not drawn yet".

Uniques
-------

They get tiles here, and the objection in ../README does not apply.  That
objection is about *portraits*: a borrowed picture of the Mouth of Sauron
worn by Oberon says something false.  A silhouette is not a portrait -- it
says "a person, violet", which is what monster.zangband.txt says too.

What it does not do, and this is the honest limit: two monsters sharing a
`base:` and a `color:` are the same tile.  Oberon, Dworkin and Mandor are all
violet people, so all three are one violet person.  That is where a
hand-drawn shape and a per-name override in the manifest should go first.

Three things the data made necessary
------------------------------------

Each of these was found by looking at the output, and each is now data rather
than a special case in the code.

*Four tones, not three.*  Terrain needs a tone for identity and a tone for the
light.  With three, grass (a dark green) and trees (a light green) landed on
the same lit colour and the wilderness became one flat field.

*A per-feature bias.*  Angband's colours were chosen for single glyphs.  FLOOR
is White and GRANITE is Light Slate, so a floor spread over a whole cell came
out *brighter* than the wall around it.  One global bias cannot fix that, so a
`feat:` line may carry a fourth field and FLOOR carries `+2`.

*A shallower ramp for traps.*  A trap you have found is a thing to spot, not
ground to walk over, so `trap-lighting:` sits at zero for a visible trap and
only dims a remembered one.

What --check measures
---------------------

A tile is one colour on black, so a colour's contrast against black *is* its
legibility -- and the bar differs by role.  A creature, object or visible trap
must be spotted (3:1).  Lit terrain must read as ground and should lose to
whatever stands on it (1.5:1).  Anything `dark` is remembered rather than seen
and has no floor at all: dim is what it means.

It also reads the sheet back and confirms every coordinate the prf names holds
ink of the colour that row is supposed to be, and that every name in the file
resolves against lib/gamedata.  Those checks exist because all three of the
ways this goes wrong are silent:

* the renderer takes the cell index modulo the sheet size rather than
  complaining, so a wrong coordinate draws a different picture;
* `lookup_monster()` falls back to a substring match, so a misspelled name
  binds to whatever contains it;
* `lookup_sval()` compares against `obj_desc_name_format()` of each kind's
  name, so `& Small wooden chest~` resolves to nothing at all -- which it did,
  for all 157 object lines, until the formatter was ported into this script.

Two more were found the same way, both about how a colour is written down.
Names are spelled four ways across the data files -- "Light Green", "Light
dark", "L_DARK", "ORANGE" -- so they are folded before lookup rather than
matched exactly; one potion flavour was missing on top of that because `T`
(Light Teal) had no mapping at all.  And trap.txt gives most of its colours as
single-letter codes but five of them as full names ("light slate"), so a
resolver that took only codes dropped those five without a word.

The check that catches this class of thing is the cheapest one here: every
shape named in the manifest must end up in the sheet.  A shape that was drawn
and never used means all of its lines were dropped somewhere, and that is how
the five traps were found.

The palette
-----------

Ten hues, four tones, in `palette.txt` with the mapping from the game's 28
colour codes.  Influence: Jeff Minter / Llamasoft.  Hyper-saturated, and
legible only because a tile carries one of them at a time -- the constraint is
what pays for the palette.

Three pairs collapse -- Slate/Light Slate, Dark/Light Dark, Mustard/Mud -- each
a pair the eye already struggles with in ASCII.  The other twenty-two codes
survive intact.

Not done yet
------------

* Per-name shapes for the named characters, starting with the Amber princes.
* Colour cycling.  Under this layout, cycling a tile's hue is arithmetic on
  the row: hold the column, rotate the colour within its block.  The cycle
  table already exists (226 definitions in lib/gamedata/visuals.txt, driven by
  visuals_cycler_get_attr_for_race), and it is switched off in graphics mode
  by one condition in ui-display.c's idle_update().  That is engine work, not
  tileset work, and belongs in its own change.
